; Kernel names resolved from KERNELBASE.dll instead of the apiset the SDK
; import libraries route them through.
;
; xwin's kernel32.lib routes EncodePointer and DecodePointer through
; api-ms-win-core-util-l1-1-0.dll, an apiset the Xbox app container does not
; provide: the package installs and the loader then fails the launch with
; 0x80070002, naming nothing. The static CRT reaches for these two in its /O2
; initialisation paths, so any optimised --uwp build can pick the import up
; without a line of application code.
;
; The same SDK routes a further pair through minor-version apisets a Series S
; does not expose either — observed on xllama (216 TU, store CRT): the
; crossbuilt PE imported api-ms-win-core-file-l1-2-2 (AreFileApisANSI) and
; api-ms-win-core-processthreads-l1-1-1 (IsProcessorFeaturePresent) and
; refused activation as 0x80270300, while the CI MSVC PE of the same app
; imports neither and launches. The CRT reaches for both with no application
; code involved.
;
; KERNELBASE.dll exports every name below, verified against the console
; itself: the export table read out of a crash dump's mapped KERNELBASE
; (Xbox OS 26100.8866), not out of an SDK import library — kernelbase.lib
; understates the DLL. The unwinder's RtlCaptureContext family is NOT here:
; the console's KERNELBASE does not export it (same dump), it belongs to
; appcontainer-ntdll.def.
;
; build.sh turns this into an import library with llvm-dlltool and places it
; before kernel32.lib, so these names resolve here and everything else stays
; where xwin put it.
LIBRARY KERNELBASE.dll
EXPORTS
    EncodePointer
    DecodePointer
    AreFileApisANSI
    IsProcessorFeaturePresent
